PART I — GOVERNING PROJECT PRINCIPLES

1. Master Objective

Build ZClone as an independent Android APK inspection, customization, repackaging, and management application powered by FMSCloneEngine.

Build FMS as an independent and reusable software library ecosystem that can serve multiple applications and projects.

ZClone is one consumer of FMS. FMS must not be architecturally restricted to ZClone.

The project must provide real, testable functionality rather than UI-only demonstrations, dummy controls, or unsupported compatibility claims.

2. Non-Negotiable Principles

1. Preserve existing verified work.
2. Inspect the actual workspace before modifying anything.
3. Do not assume unavailable files, repositories, credentials, or cloud access.
4. Do not rebuild an existing project unnecessarily.
5. Use patch-only changes when an existing implementation is available and suitable.
6. Do not claim implementation or verification without evidence.
7. Do not silently omit documented features.
8. Do not use a third-party cloning engine as the core.
9. Develop independent FMS APIs, namespaces, and implementations.
10. External dependencies are allowed only as audited supporting components.
11. Respect Android platform security, application licensing, DRM, and access controls.
12. Maintain source-code security throughout the complete lifecycle.
13. Do not start implementation before explicit user approval.

3. Project Boundaries

Separate the architecture into:

- FMS Shared Ecosystem: reusable across projects.
- FMSCloneEngine: specialized APK processing engine.
- ZClone Application: user-facing product.
- Cloud Infrastructure: optional authentication, synchronization, and trusted backend services.
- Android Native Platform: official system APIs and platform foundations.

Generic FMS libraries must not depend on ZClone-specific classes or application logic.

