17. Non-Root Android Technical Limitations

Repackaging an APK with a different package name does not guarantee that every application will run.

Potential blockers:

- Hardcoded package references.
- Native library assumptions.
- Signature verification.
- Certificate pinning.
- Play Integrity.
- Server-side license validation.
- Split APK dependencies.
- Dynamic feature modules.
- Resource references.
- Shared UID requirements.
- Protected system permissions.

The application must not bypass security controls to force compatibility.

Repackaged APKs require signing with an appropriate key.

Re-signing changes the original signing identity and can break update compatibility or application trust relationships.

The system installer must handle installation with user confirmation.

Do not promise silent installation on ordinary unrooted devices.

18. Split APK and App Bundle Handling

Detect split APK configurations.

Determine whether:

- A standalone APK is available.
- Base and configuration splits are present.
- Required dynamic features exist.
- Repackaging is technically supported.

Unsupported combinations must be reported rather than silently processed.

19. Isolated Data Clarification

Changing the target package name normally gives the installed package a separate Android application sandbox.

This does not guarantee complete functional isolation.

Application behavior may still depend on:

- Account services.
- External storage.
- Shared content providers.
- Backend identity.
- Signature validation.
- Android inter-process communication.

Do not claim universal multi-account isolation.

