Do not ask users to paste private API keys or credentials into source code.

22. Firestore Data Model

Proposed structure:

users/{userId}

- profile
- license
- cloneConfigurations
- themePresets
- deviceRegistry
- securityReports
- syncMetadata

All user records must be isolated by authenticated UID.

Firestore Security Rules must validate:

- Authenticated identity.
- Ownership.
- Allowed fields.
- Data types.
- Document size.
- Permitted operations.

Client-side rules alone must not be trusted for privileged license enforcement or device quota enforcement.

Use trusted backend verification for sensitive enforcement operations.

23. Device Registry

Store only necessary device metadata:

- Random installation UUID.
- User-controlled device label.
- Available model information.
- Android version.
- Registration time.
- Last synchronization.
- Session status.

Do not store restricted hardware identifiers.

A random installation UUID is not guaranteed to remain stable after application data removal or reinstall.

24. Cloud Synchronization

Synchronize:

- Clone configuration metadata.
- Theme presets.
- User preferences where appropriate.
- Device registration metadata.
- Security scan summaries.

Do not upload complete APK files or sensitive application contents by default.

Provide:

- Offline mode.
- Retry handling.
- Conflict detection.
- Conflict resolution.
- Sync state.
- User-controlled backup and restore.
- Account deletion and data deletion workflow.

Security reports must not contain unnecessary private data.

25. Firebase App Check and Backend Security

Use Firebase App Check where supported.

Use trusted backend services for:

- License validation.
- Device quota enforcement.
- Privileged operations.
- Server-side consistency checks.

Never trust client-supplied tier or device-limit values.

